  "title": "Convert to polar: a full disk",
  "prompt": "Convert $\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{2} \\int_{-\\sqrt{4 - x^2}}^{\\sqrt{4 - x^2}} e^{-(x^2 + y^2)}\\,dy\\,dx$ to polar coordinates, integrating $dr$ first, then $d\\theta$ (don't evaluate).",
  "answer_format": "An iterated integral like int(int(f, y, a, b), x, c, d), inner integral first. Don't evaluate it. Use r and theta.",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "tags": [
    "double integrals",
    "polar coordinates"
  ],
  "data": {
    "integrand": "e^(-(x^2 + y^2))",
    "region": [
      [
        "y",
        "-sqrt(4 - x^2)",
        "sqrt(4 - x^2)"
      ],
      [
        "x",
        "-2",
        "2"
      ]
    ],
    "order": [
      "r",
      "theta"
    ],
    "coordinates": "polar"
  },
  "answer": "int(int(e^(-r^2) r, r, 0, 2), theta, 0, 2pi)",
  "mistakes": [
    {
      "answer": "int(int(e^(-r^2) r, r, 0, 4), theta, 0, 2pi)",
      "hint": "The circle x² + y² = 4 has radius 2, so r goes up to 2, not 4."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "What region do the limits describe? Sketch y = ±√(4 − x²)."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "The limits describe the whole disk $x^2 + y^2 \\le 4$: $0 \\le r \\le 2$, $0 \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi$.",
    "$e^{-(x^2+y^2)} = e^{-r^2}$ and $dA = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$: $\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^2 e^{-r^2}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$."
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "openstax-calc3",
    "section": "5.3 Double Integrals in Polar Coordinates",
    "note": "Original problem."
  },
